  "title": "Concept Allocation Zone: Tracking How Concepts Form Across Transformer Depth",
  "summary": "This paper explores how concepts form across a transformer's residual stream, with a CAZ (Concept Allocation Zone) that represents the depth where a concept becomes separable. The framework provides a three-layer metric for measuring concept formation, and it identifies subtle allocation regions that are not easily detected. The study highlights the importance of the CAZ and its role in improving model performance through instance-based methods.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "statement": "A Concept Allocation Zone (CAZ) is not a concept but the depth segment where a model organizes geometry to make one separable.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "The CAZ is frequently multimodal, with subtle allocation regions ('gentle CAZes') that are invisible to standard peak detection.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "The framework generates seven testable predictions and provides a method to detect and understand these subtle regions.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    }
  ],
